    "narrative": "The **Austin Waves** lineup for June 20th leans heavily into high-floor consistency, led by **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (72.88) and **Jacob Wilson** (69.83). **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** remains our anchor against a favorable matchup, benefiting from a +13% skill adjustment against Rea. Meanwhile, **Jacob Wilson** receives a massive +10% park boost, making him a prime candidate for multi-hit potential. We are also capitalizing on **Andy Pages** (67.66) and **Sam Antonacci** (63.14); **Andy Pages** is particularly intriguing as his score is bolstered by a +2% speed-focused adjustment against a pitcher with a notably slow delivery, increasing his stolen base expectancy\u2014a critical factor for our 5x5 Roto standing.\n\nWe face a significant tactical challenge against elite arms today, most notably **Cole Carrigg**, who draws a tough assignment against Skenes. Despite the talent gap, **Cole Carrigg** remains in the lineup due to a substantial +14% park factor that keeps his floor near the 50.00 threshold. In terms of our bench decisions, **Miguel Vargas** (61.26) was the most difficult exclusion. While his score is strong, the density of high-efficiency starts in our middle infield and outfield forced his move to the bench; he lacks the specific platoon leverage or elite stolen base upside provided by **Nolan Schanuel** (62.14) or **Sam Antonacci** in today's specific conditions.\n\nRisk management is central to today\u2019s strategy, specifically regarding **Gunnar Henderson** (62.29). While he remains a mandatory start, his matchup against Yamamoto is our most dangerous individual encounter of the day. The -17% skill adjustment against the opposing pitcher is mitigated by his elite dynamic platoon edge, but we are monitoring the game closely to ensure his production holds. We are also keeping an eye on **Drake Baldwin**, whose 50.98 score sits just above our marginal threshold; his reliance on batting second keeps him in play, but he remains the most volatile component of our active roster.",
